# REGRESSION — an element read out with `v[i]` survives the collection
# growing past a reallocation, and does so the same way whether the
# collection is named directly or through an alias.
#
#     String s = v[0]          # rc 139 SIGSEGV before this was fixed
#     String s = alias[0]      # rc 0 -- for a reason unrelated to safety
#
# CELL: producer = the INDEX-LOAD element borrow (`v[i]`), rather than the
# `.get(i).unwrap()` chain the sibling `cow_alias_spelled_*` fixtures use.
#
# The var-decl eagerly clones the index-load result, so `s` already owns its
# bytes and needs no rescue. The defect was on the OTHER side: the anonymous
# index-load temp is tagged as a borrow into the collection, and the
# severance walk then "materialized" it on every iteration -- cloning FROM a
# pointer the previous iteration's realloc had already freed. The clone
# landed in a throwaway local nobody reads, so the work was pure loss AND a
# use-after-free. Severance now skips anonymous temps, which is the rule its
# sibling case (field borrows) already applied for the identical reason.
#
# Both spellings are asserted below because they were asymmetric before:
# the direct spelling crashed, and the alias spelling survived only because
# a mismatched collection identity meant the severance walk never found the
# temp at all -- an accidental green over the same defect.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(mk("hel", "lo"))
    Vector[String] alias = v
    String direct = v[0]
    String spelled = alias[0]
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        v.push(mk("fill", "er"))
        i = i + 1
    print(direct)
    print(spelled)
